<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.LoadTests — L2 control-plane load-test project.

    Created by customer-provisioning-orchestration-r1 task 062 (Wave C5 Batch 4E)
    per POML acceptance for spec.md FR-22 + SC #20.

    SCOPE:
      Three scenarios (POML acceptance criteria):
        1. EnqueueLatencyScenario — N=50 concurrent POST /api/runs; p50/p95/p99
           latency and 100% 202-response verification (FR-22 enqueue-and-return
           <100ms invariant).
        2. LongHandlerScenario — a synthetic long-running handler; verifies the
           caller receives 202 immediately AND that GET /api/runs/{id} continues
           to work while the handler is "in flight"; NO HTTP timeout on caller
           regardless of handler duration (FR-22 fire-and-forget invariant).
        3. ReconcilerConcurrencyScenario — N=3..5 StateReconcilerService
           instances processing M concurrent runs; verifies EXACTLY ONE distinct
           Service Bus message survives per (ready-handler, run) pair via
           MessageId dedup (SC #20 concurrency invariant).

    FRAMEWORK CHOICE (task 062 deviation):
      xUnit + Parallel.ForEachAsync + WebApplicationFactory<Program>.
      Not NBomber / k6 / BenchmarkDotNet.

      Rationale:
        - The POML permits NBomber, k6, BenchmarkDotNet, or a dotnet-based
          custom harness. The harness here is a dotnet-based custom harness.
        - Adding NBomber (~10 MB test-only package + Serilog transitive
          drift + a distinct DSL) would inflate the test-project surface for
          three narrowly-scoped scenarios that map cleanly to xUnit +
          Parallel.ForEachAsync + Stopwatch-derived percentiles.
        - Percentile computation is trivial in .NET: sort + index.
        - The in-memory seams pattern from Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Tests
          (task 057 RunsEndpointsTests) is the correct fit — a live HTTP
          load-shape against L2's own Program.cs composition, minus the
          Cosmos + Service Bus wire (which would require a real dev cluster
          to sustain 50 concurrent requests).
        - The scenarios verify the L2 REST layer's OWN latency + the
          reconciler's OWN dedup contract — the network round-trip to real
          Cosmos + Service Bus is a separate concern owned by
          Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.NightlyTests / SmokeTests.

    30-MINUTE HANDLER COMPRESSION (task 062 deviation):
      LongHandlerScenario tests at wall-clock T = 3 seconds — NOT 30 minutes.
      The invariant proven is "the L2 REST API never blocks on handler
      execution": POST /api/runs returns 202 immediately (measured); GET /api/runs
      continues to work while the "handler" is running (measured); no HTTP
      timeout is observed (measured).

      This invariant is DURATION-INDEPENDENT — the caller's HTTP socket
      closes at the 202 return; handler runtime happens off-socket. A 30-min
      production handler and a 3-second synthetic handler prove the SAME
      invariant. Compressing to 3 sec keeps CI + dev iteration honest without
      changing what is measured (see notes/l2-load-test-2026-08-18.md for the
      full equivalence argument).

    KEEP-PATH CATEGORIZATION:
      This project sits at tests/integration/{ProjectName}/ (sibling of
      Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.NightlyTests + Spe.Integration.Tests +
      Sprk.Bff.Api.IntegrationTests) — NOT under a KEEP category directory.
      This is intentional and matches the existing convention for integration
      *project* organization; the KEEP category directories at
      tests/integration/{auth,regression,data-mutation,tenant,contract,seam}/
      are for individual test FILES organized by acceptance-invariant type,
      not for test PROJECT organization.

      The scenarios themselves defend concrete acceptance criteria (FR-22
      SC #20) and are maintain-class per ADR-038 §7 build-vs-maintain — they
      are the acceptance gate for Phase F E2E acceptance and cannot be
      deleted without breaking that gate.

    ADR-038 alignment:
      - No Mock<HttpMessageHandler>.
      - No DI-registration-only tests.
      - No ctor-null-check tests.
      - Uses TimeProvider (custom test double) in the ReconcilerConcurrencyScenario;
        no Stopwatch inside production or fake production code — Stopwatch usage
        is ONLY in the test-harness measurement layer, which is idiomatic for
        latency percentile computation (parity with RunsEndpointsTests's
        Latency spot-check Stopwatch usage).

    Publish: IsPackable=false; test-only assembly. Not deployed. §10 hygiene
    (BFF publish-size ceiling ≤60 MB) is UNAFFECTED — L2-only.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!--
      TreatWarningsAsErrors=true is inherited from Directory.Build.props.
      Sibling test projects (Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Tests) do NOT
      opt out; this project matches that posture so nullable/analyzer
      warnings on the harness itself are caught before merge.
    -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="FluentAssertions" Version="6.12.0" />

    <!-- Pinned to Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions 10.0.9 transitive
         floor — mirrors Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Tests to prevent
         NU1605 downgrade under TreatWarningsAsErrors=true. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.9" />

    <!-- Options binding — the reconciler scenario constructs
         Options.Create(new ReconcilerOptions()) directly.
         Version pinned to match sibling Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Tests
         (10.0.9). -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" Version="10.0.9" />

    <!-- WebApplicationFactory<Program> — required to bring the real L2
         Program.cs composition up in-process for scenarios 1 and 2. Pinned
         to net10 framework version. Program is exposed as `public partial
         class Program` at the bottom of Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Api/
         Program.cs (was Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane/Program.cs pre-task-100
         split). -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ProjectReferences ADDED by task 100 (DS-3 Option 2 L2 project split, 2026-08-19):
      This csproj shipped WITHOUT ProjectReferences at e1959426b (task 062) —
      the LoadTest scenarios (EnqueueLatencyScenario, LongHandlerScenario,
      ReconcilerConcurrencyScenario) use L2 types (Concurrency/, Enqueue/,
      Models/, Reconciler/) directly + WebApplicationFactory<Program>. Task 100
      adds the two references the harness needs:
        - Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Core: Concurrency/, Enqueue/, Models/,
          Reconciler/ types (all in Core namespace-preserved paths).
        - Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Api: WebApplicationFactory<Program>
          resolves to the .Api host's composition root (401/403/202 REST
          scenarios need the full JWT + endpoint pipeline).
    Without these the sln build breaks on this project post-split. The pre-split
    LoadTests compiled via the (now-removed) monolithic
    Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane project.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\src\server\services\Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Core\Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\src\server\services\Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Api\Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Api.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>


  <!-- Shared test helpers (TestSessionOwner, TestHttpContexts, TestTokenCredential).
       Linked rather than copied so every test project sees ONE definition; a second copy is how
       two projects drift into disagreeing about what a test callers oid is.

       ..\Shared\Dataverse\** is EXCLUDED here, and only here, of the four projects that link this
       folder. Those helpers need Moq + Microsoft.Xrm.Sdk + Spaarke.Dataverse; this project is the
       one that has none of them (it references only the Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane projects,
       so it does not even get Xrm/Dataverse transitively the way the Sprk.Bff.Api-referencing
       suites do). Adding those packages to a provisioning load-test project to satisfy a glob
       would be the wrong trade.

       Why a SUBFOLDER rather than a per-file Compile Remove: a file-name exclusion silently rots
       the moment someone adds a second Dataverse-dependent helper — they get an error in a project
       they have never opened, with no hint why. The folder makes the dependency boundary the thing
       you name, so the next helper lands on the correct side of it by placement.
       Introduced 2026-09-01 (#858) after TestActingUserBusinessUnit broke this project's build. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\Shared\**\*.cs" LinkBase="SharedTestHelpers" />
    <Compile Remove="..\Shared\Dataverse\**\*.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
